  "body": "# Associated User Story\n<br>\nAs an officer, I want a map or list or something easy to put a location for an event rather than latitue and longitude.\n\n\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n<br>\n\n\n**When creating or editing an event, officers can choose a location by searching an address or place name, picking from a saved list, or clicking on a map, without entering latitude and longitude.**\n\n- Sunny: An officer creating an event starts typing a place name or address, sees matching suggestions, and selects one. A pin appears on a map to confirm the spot, and the officer can also drag the pin or choose from a list of commonly used locations (e.g., campus buildings, the PT clinic). They save the event, and the location displays as a readable name or address for members, with the coordinates stored in the background.\n\n- Rainy: The search finds no results, the map fails to load, or the officer tries to save an event with no location selected. The system shows a clear message (e.g., \"No locations found, try a different search\" or \"The map could not be loaded, choose from the list or try again later\") instead of a blank map or a confusing error. The officer can still pick from the saved list, an event is never saved with a missing or incorrect location, and only officers can add or edit event locations.\n\nDefinition of Done -\nA story is done when its acceptance criteria are met and every item in the team PR template is satisfied:\n(A) code review by a non-author, coding standard met, RuboCop checked with output/CI link, and setup changes documented;\n(B) the human-only maintainability checks (meaningful comments and names, skinny controllers, thin views, DRY, Rails conventions, foreign keys, enums, nested routes);\n(C) tests added or updated and passing. Any deferred items or RuboCop exclusions are explained in Section D of the PR.\n\n\n",
